"confidence":0.58},{"name":"Broadway","confidence":0.55}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"5c02c8aa7f9b1bf7fa578d7c7ff94f6c9ace0578fbc876858c51e4f120df7602","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the art form of tap dancing and its cultural significance, fitting best within the framework of Arts & Criticism, while also addressing its societal context.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"3d681ec649d2f0bd7aea40d50b2832b6093346027c500f39be62006dd1f7005c","summary":"The first authoritative history of tap dancing, one of the great art forms--along with jazz and musical comedy--created in America What the Eye Hears offers an authoritative account of the great American art of tap dancing. Brian Seibert, a dance critic for The New York Times, begins by exploring tap's origins as a hybrid of the jig and clog dancing and dances brought from Africa by slaves. He tracks tap's transfer to the stage through blackface minstrelsy and charts its growth as a cousin to jazz in the vaudeville circuits. Seibert chronicles tap's spread to ubiquity on Broadway and in Hollywood, analyzes its decline after World War II, and celebrates its rediscovery and reinvention by new generations of American and international performers. In the process, we discover how the history of tap dancing is central to any meaningful account of American popular culture. This is a story with a huge cast of characters, from Master Juba through Bill Robinson and Shirley Temple, Fred Astaire and Ginger Rogers, and Gene Kelly and Paul Draper to Gregory Hines and Savion Glover.","publicationYear":2016,"displaySummary":"The first authoritative history of tap dancing, in America What the Eye Hears offers an authoritative account of the great American art of tap dancing. Brian Seibert, a dance critic for The New York Times, begins by exploring tap's origins as a hybrid of the jig and clog dancing and dances brought from Africa by slaves. He tracks tap's transfer to the stage through blackface minstrelsy and charts its growth as a cousin to jazz in the vaudeville circuits. Seibert chronicles tap's spread to ubiquity on Broadway and in Hollywood, analyzes its decline after World War II, and celebrates its rediscovery and reinvention by new generations of American and international performers. In the process, we discover how the history of tap dancing is central to any meaningful account of American popular culture. This is a story with a huge cast of characters, from Master Juba through Bill Robinson and Shirley Temple, Fred Astaire and Ginger Rogers, and Gene Kelly and Paul Draper to Gregory Hines and Savion Glover.","primaryTopic":"Artistic Biography","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Arts & Criticism","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Arts & Criticism","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the art form of tap dancing and its cultural significance, fitting best within the framework of Arts & Criticism, while also addressing its societal context.","id":"subject-evidence-book-what-the-eye-hears-a-history-of-tap-dancing-brian-seibert-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Performing Arts","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-what-the-eye-hears-a-history-of-tap-dancing-brian-seibert","id":"subject-evidence-book-what-the-eye-hears-a-history-of-tap-dancing-brian-seibert-3"},{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":2,"confidence":"low","note":"American history keyword; world history keyword; history keyword or his